## Required Output Contract
You MUST return valid JSON with exactly two fields:
```json
{
  "reasoning": "1-2 sentence explanation in English",
  "content": "<div class=\"flex justify-center...\">...</div>"
}
```
- `reasoning` must be English, regardless of activity language.
- `content` must be complete HTML starting with the wrapper below.

## Layout Shell (MANDATORY)
Use this exact outer shell:
```html
<div class="flex justify-center items-start min-h-[calc(100dvh-100px)]">
  <div class="container mx-auto max-w-5xl bg-white rounded-lg px-24 max-lg:px-12 max-sm:px-6 pt-12 pb-12" id="content">
    <!-- one section goes here -->
  </div>
</div>
```

## Critical Validation Rules (MUST PASS)
1. Include exactly ONE `<section>` inside `#content`.
2. The section must have ALL of these attributes exactly:
   - `data-section-type` equal to the provided section type
   - `data-section-id` equal to the provided section ID
   - no `role="activity"` attribute
3. Use ONLY the provided `data-id` values (text IDs and image IDs). Never invent IDs.
4. Every provided text ID must appear exactly once in one element's `data-id`.
5. Every image ID used in HTML must be from the provided image list.
6. Text for each text ID must match the provided text exactly (no paraphrasing, no punctuation/case changes).
7. Do not output visible raw text outside an element that has a valid provided `data-id`. The only exception is decorative punctuation already included inside a valid text element.
8. No markdown, no code fences in `content`, and no additional top-level fields.
9. For text leaves, default to `<span>`. Use block elements (`<p>`, `<h1>`–`<h6>`, `<li>`) ONLY when the leaf is semantically a standalone block — a full paragraph of prose, a heading, a list item, or preformatted content.
10. Every selectable inline segment MUST carry `class="activity-underline-option"` and a unique `data-activity-item`.
11. Every selectable inline segment MUST also carry `data-question-group="question-group-N"` so multiple underline questions in one section stay independent.
12. Every provided text leaf must remain visible exactly once as its own `data-id` wrapper. Do not drop text IDs, do not duplicate them, and do not merge two different text IDs into one element.
13. If the instruction asks the learner to underline a word, phrase, or grammatical category inside a sentence, the sentence text MUST contain one or more inline selectable spans with class="activity-underline-option". Plain static sentence text with no selectable spans is invalid.
14. For word-level questions, wrap EVERY word of the sentence in its own inline element with class="activity-underline-option", a unique `data-activity-item`, and that question's `data-question-group`. Only number markers like "(i)", example labels like "Mfano:", and punctuation stay outside the selectable spans. For phrase- or sentence-level questions, wrap each candidate phrase or sentence the same way.
15. Never leave the answer surface fully static when the learner is expected to click text directly.
16. Every selectable inline segment MUST contain visible text. The runtime derives each segment's screen-reader name from its own text content, so an empty segment is announced as an unlabeled checkbox. This is validated and will fail.
17. Keep all selectable segments of one question inside a single shared ancestor (normally that question's `data-id` text wrapper). The runtime labels that ancestor "Question N" so assistive tech can tell which sentence a segment belongs to; segments of one question scattered across unrelated wrappers break that grouping.

## Underline Activity Structure
- This activity lets the learner click/tap inline text directly.
- A selectable segment may be:
  - one word
  - several separate words
  - one phrase
  - one full sentence
- REQUIRED: if the learner is supposed to underline text inside a sentence, the rendered HTML for that sentence must contain one or more descendant elements with class `activity-underline-option`. If a sentence is rendered as plain text only, the output is invalid.
- IMPORTANT: when the page includes a worked example such as "Mfano:", treat that example as supporting context only unless the textbook clearly presents it as an interactive question too. By default, base the selectable answer units on the actual activity questions/instructions, not on the worked example.
- Use the TEXT ITSELF as the answer surface. Do not convert the activity into radio cards, checkboxes, or external option rows.
- Wrap the ORIGINAL text leaf in one element with its provided `data-id`, then place selectable inline spans INSIDE it. This preserves the exact source text while making chosen segments interactive.
- Keep non-selectable text as plain inline text inside the same `data-id` wrapper.
- Keep each source text leaf in its own `data-id` wrapper. If numbering like "(i)" is a separate text leaf from the sentence, keep them as separate elements with separate `data-id`s.
- Use `activity-underline-option inline rounded px-1 cursor-pointer transition-colors underline-offset-4 decoration-2` on every selectable segment.
- IMPORTANT: do not wrap only the correct answer, and do not curate a subset of "plausible" words. For word-level questions wrap EVERY word in the sentence — in the printed book the learner can underline any word, so every word must be selectable here too. The answer key determines which segments are correct.
- If the instruction asks for one word, make every word in the sentence selectable, even though only one is correct.
- If the instruction asks for several words, make every word selectable, and let multiple of them be correct in the answer key.
- If the intended answer unit is a WORD, do NOT make the whole sentence selectable just because the sentence contains that word. The selectable segment must match the answer unit.
- For grammar-style tasks such as "underline the pronoun", "underline the noun", "underline the adjective", or "underline the verb", the selectable units must be the individual candidate words or short phrases inside the sentence — NOT the whole sentence.
- If the instruction asks for one sentence, make each sentence a separate selectable segment; do not merge the whole paragraph into one selectable block.
- If the instruction asks for a phrase, wrap that phrase as one selectable segment, but keep neighboring phrases or sentences as separate selectable segments where they are plausible alternatives.
- Only use sentence-level selectable segments when the instruction explicitly asks the learner to underline the correct sentence or sentences.
- Do not make every sentence a selectable segment just because each sentence contains a correct word. The selectable segment must match the intended answer unit: word, phrase, or sentence.
- Prefer smaller meaningful selectable units over oversized ones. Do not wrap an entire paragraph as one segment unless the textbook truly asks the learner to underline the entire paragraph.
- Do not invent extra text such as "Tap here" or "Choose below".

## Segmentation Decision Rules (VERY IMPORTANT)
- First decide the intended answer unit from the instruction:
  - asks for a pronoun / noun / verb / adjective / word / letter / number inside a sentence → selectable units must be word-level or short phrase-level
  - asks for a phrase → selectable units must be phrase-level
  - asks for a sentence / correct sentence / sentences → selectable units must be sentence-level
- After deciding the answer unit, keep ALL selectable spans at that same level within the question. Do not mix sentence-level spans with word-level spans in the same question unless the page truly presents mixed mechanics.
- For word-level questions, EVERY word of the sentence is a selectable unit — never a curated subset. The only text left plain is number markers, example labels, and punctuation.
- For sentence-based textbook exercises where each numbered line is one sentence and the learner must underline a word class inside each sentence, each numbered line is a QUESTION, but the selectable units inside that question are WORDS, not the whole numbered sentence.
- Anti-patterns you MUST avoid:
  - wrapping an entire sentence when the learner is supposed to underline only one word in that sentence
  - wrapping every sentence as one option for grammar-identification tasks
  - turning the worked example into the main interactive target when the real numbered questions come after it
  - making only the correct word selectable
  - wrapping only a curated subset of words (for example subject + verb + object) and leaving the other words as plain text

## Grammar-Identification Default
- When the instruction is of the form "underline the X in the following sentences" and X is a word class or grammatical category, default to this pattern:
  - each numbered sentence is its own question group
  - EVERY word inside that sentence is selectable, each as its own span
  - the entire sentence is NOT selectable as one unit
  - the answer key decides which word or words are correct
  - if the number marker (such as "(i)") is a separate text leaf, keep it outside the selectable sentence text and preserve its own `data-id`
- For grammar-identification exercises such as "Underline the pronoun in each sentence", each sentence is a question group, but the selectable units inside that sentence must be word-level or short phrase-level spans.
- English example:
  - Sentence: "She reads books every day."
  - Correct answer unit: the word "She"
  - Valid pattern:
    - every word — "She", "reads", "books", "every", "day" — is wrapped in its own `.activity-underline-option` span; the final period stays outside the spans
    - the full sentence must NOT be one large selectable span
    - the sentence must NOT remain plain static text with no selectable spans
    - wrapping only some of the words (for example just "She", "reads", "books") is INVALID
- Invalid pattern example:
  - Sentence: "She reads books every day."
  - Outputting the entire sentence as plain text with no `.activity-underline-option` spans is invalid.

## Text-ID Preservation Rules (VERY IMPORTANT)
- Do not merge two different provided text IDs into one HTML element.
- Do not move a separate numbering label, title, instruction, or example label into the sentence element.
- Only add `.activity-underline-option` spans inside the text leaf that actually contains the selectable sentence or phrase.
- Titles, instructions, example labels like "Mfano:", and numbering markers like "(i)" are normally NOT selectable unless the page explicitly makes them the answer surface.

## Example Pattern
```html
<section class="section mb-8" data-section-type="SECTION_TYPE_FROM_INPUT" data-section-id="SECTION_ID_FROM_INPUT">
  <div class="space-y-6">
    <!-- This example assumes TEXT_ID_1 is an activity_instruction, not a document heading. -->
    <p class="font-bold text-blue-700" data-id="TEXT_ID_1">Underline the pronoun.</p>
    <p class="text-2xl leading-relaxed">
      <span data-id="TEXT_ID_2">
        <span class="activity-underline-option inline rounded px-1 cursor-pointer transition-colors underline-offset-4 decoration-2" data-activity-item="item-1" data-question-group="question-group-1">Mimi</span>
        <span class="activity-underline-option inline rounded px-1 cursor-pointer transition-colors underline-offset-4 decoration-2" data-activity-item="item-2" data-question-group="question-group-1">ninacheza</span>
        <span class="activity-underline-option inline rounded px-1 cursor-pointer transition-colors underline-offset-4 decoration-2" data-activity-item="item-3" data-question-group="question-group-1">mpira</span>.
      </span>
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
```

## Required Word-Level Pattern
When the instruction asks the learner to underline a word inside a sentence, follow this exact structural idea:

```html
<p class="text-2xl leading-relaxed">
  <span data-id="TEXT_ID_SENTENCE">
    <span class="activity-underline-option inline rounded px-1 cursor-pointer transition-colors underline-offset-4 decoration-2" data-activity-item="item-1" data-question-group="question-group-1">She</span>
    <span class="activity-underline-option inline rounded px-1 cursor-pointer transition-colors underline-offset-4 decoration-2" data-activity-item="item-2" data-question-group="question-group-1">reads</span>
    <span class="activity-underline-option inline rounded px-1 cursor-pointer transition-colors underline-offset-4 decoration-2" data-activity-item="item-3" data-question-group="question-group-1">books</span>
    <span class="activity-underline-option inline rounded px-1 cursor-pointer transition-colors underline-offset-4 decoration-2" data-activity-item="item-4" data-question-group="question-group-1">every</span>
    <span class="activity-underline-option inline rounded px-1 cursor-pointer transition-colors underline-offset-4 decoration-2" data-activity-item="item-5" data-question-group="question-group-1">day</span>.
  </span>
</p>
```

This is VALID because EVERY word of the sentence is its own selectable span (punctuation stays outside the spans).

Wrapping only some of the words is INVALID for word-level questions:

```html
<p class="text-2xl leading-relaxed">
  <span data-id="TEXT_ID_SENTENCE">
    <span class="activity-underline-option inline rounded px-1 cursor-pointer transition-colors underline-offset-4 decoration-2" data-activity-item="item-1" data-question-group="question-group-1">She</span>
    <span class="activity-underline-option inline rounded px-1 cursor-pointer transition-colors underline-offset-4 decoration-2" data-activity-item="item-2" data-question-group="question-group-1">reads</span>
    books every day.
  </span>
</p>
```

The following is INVALID for this activity type because it contains no clickable underline options:

```html
<p class="text-2xl leading-relaxed">
  <span data-id="TEXT_ID_SENTENCE">She reads books every day.</span>
</p>
```

## Key Requirements
- Keep the layout close to the textbook.
- Make selectable text visibly look tappable, but not pre-selected.
- A word-level sentence will contain one span per word inside its `data-id` text wrapper — that is expected and required.
- Every word is selectable, but only the answer key decides which are correct.
- For multiple questions, use `question-group-1`, `question-group-2`, etc.
